./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:233:3554,Deep Learning for Accurate Diagnosis of Viral Infections through scRNA-seq Analysis: A Comprehensive Benchmark Study,deep learning for accurate diagnosis of viral infections through scrnaseq analysis a comprehensive benchmark study,,,,,"This outcome can be attributed to scGPT’s pretraining process As a result, when applied to  our dataset, scGPT prioritized the  While scGPT’s ability to discern cell types even in a disease-","['Z Yang', 'X Chen', 'B Zhu', 'T Chen']",2025.0,Journal of Data-centric …,,https://openreview.net/forum?id=wlH6Np0Um0,['google_scholar'],1,[],1,,,,,99,"{""paper_type"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""text_component_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""Title indicates a benchmark study; abstract mentions scRNA-seq but provides no clear natural-language text component or text-bio bridge.""}",benchmark_resource,yes,unclear,unclear,benchmark_resource,text_component_unclear,none,none,not_assessed,thin_abstract_boundary,Title indicates a benchmark study; abstract mentions scRNA-seq but provides no clear natural-language text component or text-bio bridge.,"{""paper_type"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""benchmark_resource"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The paper presents a benchmark study using scGPT for viral infection diagnosis, citing its pretraining process but does not describe any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion.""}",benchmark_resource,unclear,yes,benchmark_resource,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The paper presents a benchmark study using scGPT for viral infection diagnosis, citing its pretraining process but does not describe any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,benchmark_resource,yes,unclear,unclear,unclear,yes,benchmark_resource,text_component_unclear,,,,,"Title indicates a benchmark study; abstract mentions scRNA-seq but provides no clear natural-language text component or text-bio bridge. | The paper presents a benchmark study using scGPT for viral infection diagnosis, citing its pretraining process but does not describe any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion.",EXCLUDE,benchmark_resource,,EXCLUDE,benchmark_resource,text_component_unclear,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:234:3555,Single-Cell and Spatial Omics: Methods and Applications.,singlecell and spatial omics methods and applications,10.1002/mco2.70713,,41953641.0,,"ABSTRACT Single‐cell and spatial omics have revolutionized biomedical research by enabling high‐resolution molecular profiling across cells and tissues, thereby overcoming key limitations of bulk sequencing and revealing unprecedented cellular heterogeneity and spatial organization central to development, homeostasis, and disease. Specifically, advances in high‐throughput, subcellular, and multiomics profiling are promoting the field toward deeper insights. In parallel, computational progress, including generative artificial intelligence (AI) and foundation models, is developing rapidly for manipulating multimodal multiomics data. These advancements have been applied to diverse diseases and biological systems, facilitating innovative biomedical findings. However, a significant gap persists between rapid methodological advances and their systematic application for deciphering human biology and pathology. This review synthesizes recent breakthroughs in single‐cell and spatial technologies and surveys computational methods, including AI‐driven approaches, foundation models, and multi‐omics integration algorithms for both single‐cell and spatial analyses. We then summarize representative applications across major human organ systems in health and disease, highlighting opportunities for biomarker discovery, therapeutic target identification, and precision medicine. Finally, we discuss current challenges and future directions for bridging technological innovation with robust biomedical discovery and translational impact. This review provides a vital guide for researchers in the field, offering critical insights for accelerating the translation of single‐cell and spatial omics.",Cen Xiaoping; Huang Xiaolan; Deng Enjin; Gong Xue; Tan Na; Ye Jifeng; Wang Yin; Eils Roland; Luo Qun; Li Yixue; Qu Fangfang,2026.0,MedComm,,,"['google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'scopus', 'semantic_scholar']",4,['10.1002/mco2.70713'],4,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,100,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a review of single-cell and spatial omics methods and applications, not a primary generative text-bio model.""}",review_editorial,yes,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a review of single-cell and spatial omics methods and applications, not a primary generative text-bio model.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generative artificial intelligence (AI)"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a review that mentions generative AI and foundation models but does not describe a specific generative architecture or provide evidence that the paper's candidate model is generative or foundation-model based.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,generative artificial intelligence (AI),clear_exclude,The abstract is a review that mentions generative AI and foundation models but does not describe a specific generative architecture or provide evidence that the paper's candidate model is generative or foundation-model based.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,yes,unclear,unclear,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,,,,,,"The abstract presents a review of single-cell and spatial omics methods and applications, not a primary generative text-bio model. | The abstract is a review that mentions generative AI and foundation models but does not describe a specific generative architecture or provide evidence that the paper's candidate model is generative or foundation-model based.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:235:3556,Generative AI in drug repurposing and biomarker discovery: a multimodal approach.,generative ai in drug repurposing and biomarker discovery a multimodal approach,10.3389/fbinf.2026.1755412,,41938337.0,,"Introduction Computational drug repurposing has been widely explored using similarity-based methods, network diffusion, matrix factorization, deep learning, and graph neural networks (GNNs). However, recent heterogeneous GNN models, such as TxGNN and GAT-based models, demonstrate serious limitations for real-world biomedical applications, including poor generalization to sparsely annotated diseases, limited disease-level adaptation, and inability to effectively combine heterogeneous evidence from curated databases, multi-omics profiles, and unstructured biomedical literature. Methods This article proposes a heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network named HAMGNN, which employs three major innovations: (i) relation-sensitive multi-head attention to prioritize biologically significant interactions among heterogeneous edge types, (ii) a disease-focused meta-learning framework enabling rapid adaptation to newly observed or under-informed diseases, and (iii) a literature-enhanced knowledge graph construction pipeline encoding high-confidence, LLM-extracted therapeutic information. The model was tested on a large multimodal biomedical knowledge graph assembled from DrugBank, DisGeNET, and Hetionet, comprising more than 2.2 million edges, using a stringent disjoint disease-based (cold-start) evaluation protocol. Results HAMGNN achieved a receiver operating characteristic–area under the curve (ROC–AUC) of 0.98 and precision of 0.95, representing a 10%–15% improvement over TxGNN and GAT-GNN on unseen disease generalization. Translational applicability was demonstrated through Alzheimer’s disease and Long COVID case studies, identifying clinically plausible repurposing candidates and disease-associated biomarker signatures via mechanistic pathways. Discussion HAMGNN offers a generalized, biologically grounded, and unified framework for evidence-based drug repurposing and biomarker discovery in complex and emerging diseases.",Saranya K; Joseph Emerson Raja; Kalaiarasi Ts; Karthiga M,2026.0,Frontiers in bioinformatics,,,"['pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.3389/fbinf.2026.1755412'],2,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,101,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes HAMGNN as a GNN that uses LLM-extracted therapeutic information as side features for drug repurposing, indicating an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio generative model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,application_wrapper,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract describes HAMGNN as a GNN that uses LLM-extracted therapeutic information as side features for drug repurposing, indicating an application wrapper rather than a primary text-bio generative model.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents HAMGNN, a heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning GNN used for prediction (ROC-AUC 0.98) with no mention of decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, or other generative mechanisms.""}",primary_model_paper,no,no,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents HAMGNN, a heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning GNN used for prediction (ROC-AUC 0.98) with no mention of decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, or other generative mechanisms.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN)"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN)"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN)"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents HAMGNN, a graph neural network that uses LLM-extracted information for knowledge graph construction, with no generative component or direct text input, supporting classification as an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,no,application_wrapper,none,heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN),heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN),heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN),clear_exclude,"The abstract presents HAMGNN, a graph neural network that uses LLM-extracted information for knowledge graph construction, with no generative component or direct text input, supporting classification as an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio model.",EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,yes,no,no,no,no,application_wrapper,,heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN),heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN),heterogeneous attention-based meta-learning graph neural network (HAMGNN),clear_exclude,"The abstract presents HAMGNN, a graph neural network that uses LLM-extracted information for knowledge graph construction, with no generative component or direct text input, supporting classification as an application wrapper rather than a primary generative text-bio model.",EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:236:3557,"Artificial intelligence in oral oncology: Current advances and future potential in diagnosis, prognosis, and therapeutic decision-making.",artificial intelligence in oral oncology current advances and future potential in diagnosis prognosis and therapeutic decisionmaking,10.1016/j.ctarc.2026.101193,,41930554.0,,"Oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) poses a significant global health challenge owing to its rising incidence, late-stage diagnosis, and poor five-year survival rates. Traditional diagnostic and prognostic frameworks are limited by subjectivity, delayed turnaround times, and insufficient integration of molecular and imaging data. In this context, artificial intelligence (AI), which encompasses machine learning, deep learning, and natural language processing, has emerged as a promising tool for supporting diagnostic, prognostic, and therapeutic decision-making in oral oncology. This review summarizes current research on the application of AI in three core domains: diagnosis, prognosis, and therapeutic decision-making. Diagnostic advancements include convolutional neural network-based models for image analysis, digital pathology, and mobile screening tools, which demonstrate high accuracy in early lesion detection and tumor grading. Prognostic tools leverage multimodal data, including histopathology, radiomics, and genomic profiles, to improve risk stratification and survival prediction, often outperforming conventional staging systems in these aspects. In therapeutic planning, AI has the potential to assist in precision radiotherapy planning and surgical navigation, and predictive modeling of treatment responses through radiogenomic integration. Despite promising outcomes, widespread clinical adoption is hindered by data scarcity, model overfitting, interpretability issues, and ethical concerns regarding bias and privacy. Future directions emphasize federated learning, explainable AI, and multi-omics integration to enhance the scalability, transparency, and equity of cancer care. This review underscores the pivotal role of AI in reshaping oral oncology into a predictive, personalized, and data-driven discipline.",Annamalai Aravinth; Dhanes Vindhya; Jayalakshmi Lingaraj; Shanmugam Rajeshkumar; Ravi Sasikumar,2026.0,Cancer treatment and research communications,,,['pubmed'],1,['10.1016/j.ctarc.2026.101193'],1,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,102,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a review of AI applications in oral oncology, not a primary generative text-bio model.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a review of AI applications in oral oncology, not a primary generative text-bio model.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a review article summarizing AI applications in oral oncology without describing any generative architecture or foundation model.""}",review_editorial,no,no,review_editorial,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,The abstract is a review article summarizing AI applications in oral oncology without describing any generative architecture or foundation model.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,no,no,review_editorial,,,,,,"The abstract describes a review of AI applications in oral oncology, not a primary generative text-bio model. | The abstract is a review article summarizing AI applications in oral oncology without describing any generative architecture or foundation model.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:237:3558,AI assistance in tumor multidisciplinary teams.,ai assistance in tumor multidisciplinary teams,10.1016/j.esmorw.2026.100684,,41930313.0,,"Multidisciplinary teams (MDTs), the cornerstone of modern cancer care, are facing significant operational inefficiencies. These challenges include the laborious, manual synthesis of unstructured, multimodal patient data for case preparation, which is time-consuming and prone to information overload. Furthermore, heterogeneous operational processes across MDTs themselves compound these issues. Additionally, clinical decisions are often archived in static documents, preventing the systematic collection of decision rationales essential for continuous learning and research. We propose that artificial intelligence (AI), particularly natural language processing (NLP) and large language models (LLMs), can act as integrated partners to solve these problems. The capacity of AI to seamlessly integrate diverse datasets-including imaging, histopathology, genomics, and clinical data-may be instrumental in enhancing diagnostic accuracy, refining personalized treatment plans within a complex cancer management journey. This integration can be achieved through a tiered approach, utilizing models from small NLP for targeted information extraction to foundational generative NLP for complex evidence synthesis, while addressing key challenges in validation, ethical governance, and regulatory oversight. International initiatives are actively developing validated frameworks to facilitate the widespread and standardized adoption of these AI solutions, while taking into account heterogeneous operational processes. By improving data management, streamlining decision making, and establishing crucial feedback loops, AI integration promises to enhance patient outcomes and optimize resource utilization within cancer care.",Geukes Foppen R J; Morkūnas M; Traverso A; Dienstmann R,2026.0,ESMO real world data and digital oncology,,,['pubmed'],1,['10.1016/j.esmorw.2026.100684'],1,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,103,"{""paper_type"": ""application_wrapper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""application_wrapper"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""mixed_signals"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""natural language processing (NLP) and large language models (LLMs)"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""integrate diverse datasets-including imaging, histopathology, genomics, and clinical data"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes using existing LLMs/NLP to integrate multimodal cancer data but does not describe a new text-bio model, indicating an application wrapper rather than a primary model.""}",application_wrapper,yes,unclear,unclear,application_wrapper,mixed_signals,natural language processing (NLP) and large language models (LLMs),"integrate diverse datasets-including imaging, histopathology, genomics, and clinical data",not_assessed,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract proposes using existing LLMs/NLP to integrate multimodal cancer data but does not describe a new text-bio model, indicating an application wrapper rather than a primary model.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""foundational generative NLP"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract discusses AI and LLMs for multidisciplinary tumor teams but does not describe a new generative model; it is a review/discussion of AI assistance.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,foundational generative NLP,clear_exclude,The abstract discusses AI and LLMs for multidisciplinary tumor teams but does not describe a new generative model; it is a review/discussion of AI assistance.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""mixed_signals"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""Abstract proposes AI/NLP/LLM assistance for tumor MDTs but does not specify a new model with clear text component, text-bio bridge, or generative mechanism.""}",review_editorial,yes,unclear,unclear,unclear,yes,review_editorial,mixed_signals,none,none,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"Abstract proposes AI/NLP/LLM assistance for tumor MDTs but does not specify a new model with clear text component, text-bio bridge, or generative mechanism.",EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,yes,unclear,unclear,unclear,yes,review_editorial,mixed_signals,none,none,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"Abstract proposes AI/NLP/LLM assistance for tumor MDTs but does not specify a new model with clear text component, text-bio bridge, or generative mechanism.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,mixed_signals,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:238:3559,A deep-learning based biomarker of systemic cellular senescence burden to predict mortality and health outcomes.,a deeplearning based biomarker of systemic cellular senescence burden to predict mortality and health outcomes,10.64898/2026.03.20.26348913,,41929337.0,,"Introduction: The accumulation of senescent cells is a recognized hallmark of biological aging and is associated with the onset of multiple chronic medical conditions. Senescent cells exhibit a distinct secretory profile, known as the senescence-associated secretory phenotype (SASP), which can propagate cellular senescence to neighboring and distant tissues. Measuring SASP factors in blood serves as a practical proxy for cellular senescence burden and may help track disease states and intervention outcomes. Methods: We developed and validated a composite SASP Score by integrating large-scale population proteomics data with a semi-supervised deep learning framework. The analytical workflow included: (1) selection of biologically curated SASP proteins; (2) development of a Guided autoencoder with Transformer (GAET) model using data from the UK Biobank Pharma Proteomics Project (UKB-PPP); (3) internal evaluation and association analyses within the UK Biobank; and (4) external validation and longitudinal assessment in an independent randomized clinical trial cohort. Results: The deep learning-based SASP Score was a strong, independent predictor of mortality risk and incident serious, chronic medical conditions (e.g., dementia, COPD, myocardial infarction, stroke). In an independent cohort, multimodal exercise significantly changed the SASP Score trajectory over 18 months. Discussion: Our findings support the potential of a deep learning-derived SASP Score as a biomarker for systemic cellular senescence burden. Our statistical approach can offer enhanced interpretability and cross-platform utility, providing a valuable tool for aging research and the evaluation of geroscience-guided interventions.",Zhao Shangshu; Kuo Chia-Ling; Lenze Eric J; Wetherell Julie L; Haynes Laura; El-Ahmad Perla; Fortinsky Richard; Kuchel George; Harris Trevor; Diniz Breno S,2026.0,medRxiv : the preprint server for health sciences,,,"['pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.64898/2026.03.20.26348913'],2,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,104,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a deep learning model (GAET) applied to proteomics data for a SASP score, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or descriptions, so it lacks a substantive text component and text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a deep learning model (GAET) applied to proteomics data for a SASP score, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or descriptions, so it lacks a substantive text component and text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a Guided autoencoder with Transformer (GAET) model as a predictive biomarker for mortality and health outcomes, without mentioning any decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, or other explicit generative mechanism. It also provides no evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model characteristics.""}",primary_model_paper,no,no,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a Guided autoencoder with Transformer (GAET) model as a predictive biomarker for mortality and health outcomes, without mentioning any decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, or other explicit generative mechanism. It also provides no evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,,,,,,"The abstract describes a deep learning model (GAET) applied to proteomics data for a SASP score, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, or descriptions, so it lacks a substantive text component and text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents a Guided autoencoder with Transformer (GAET) model as a predictive biomarker for mortality and health outcomes, without mentioning any decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, or other explicit generative mechanism. It also provides no evidence of broad pretraining or foundation-model characteristics.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:239:3560,Hidden immune memory niches in inflammatory skin diseases.,hidden immune memory niches in inflammatory skin diseases,10.64898/2026.03.20.713219,,41929140.0,,"Disease-associated histopathological features are widely used to identify tissue microenvironments or niches for diagnostics and treatment response in clinical practice. However, despite its widespread use, histopathology does not reveal the full cellular and molecular composition of known pathological niches. Furthermore, the existence of pathological niches that may not be histologically discernible remains unknown. In this study, we generated a spatially-resolved multi-modal molecular atlas of ∼5 million human skin cells (including 113 skin sections profiled using Xenium-5k) and applied deep learning to unbiasedly decode 26 skin niches in health and disease. Several disease-associated niches corresponded to known histopathological features, and we defined their cellular and molecular features, co-localisations, and interactions. Additionally, we discovered an immunologically active role for skin appendageal structures in disease mechanisms, potentially contributing to inflammatory memory, that was not identifiable using standard histopathological analysis. These include a resident memory T cell-rich niche in the sebaceous gland and a plasma cell-rich niche in the sweat gland, analogous to the gland-associated immune niche in lung. Finally, we illustrate how our atlas can be used to generate high-resolution representations using transfer learning, resolving rare T cell and sebocyte subsets not possible in the original studies, validating niche identification, and the spatial enrichment of candidate genes linked to disease-associated genetic variants. Overall, our study links histopathology and atlas-scale genomics to reveal novel insights into inflammatory disease pathogenesis, chronicity, and potentially curative therapeutic avenues, using skin as an exemplar tissue for this approach.",Steele Lloyd; Foster April R; Roberts Kenny; Admane Chloe; Birk Sebastian; Mazin Pavel V; Akbarnejad Amir; Tudor Catherine; Rumney Benjamin; Chan Hon Man; Olabi Bayanne; Jiang Treasa; Rowe Victoria; Baudry David; Hale Christine; Winheim Elena; Farr Elias; McWilliam Joe; Gambardella Laure; Chakala Keerthi Priya; Gopee Nusayhah H; Binkevich Aljes; Predeus Alexander; Prete Martin; Vahidi Amirhossein; O'Toole Edel A; Basurto-Lozada Daniela; Horsfall David; Stephenson Emily; Shanmugiah Vijaya Baskar Mahalingam; Geha Raif S; Xavier Ramnik J; Smith Catherine; Mahil Satveer; Lotfollahi Mohammad; Haniffa Muzlifah,2026.0,bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology,,,"['pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.64898/2026.03.20.713219'],2,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,105,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""Abstract describes a spatially-resolved multi-modal molecular atlas and deep learning analysis of skin niches, with no mention of text/language components or generative text-bio modeling.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"Abstract describes a spatially-resolved multi-modal molecular atlas and deep learning analysis of skin niches, with no mention of text/language components or generative text-bio modeling.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""generate high-resolution representations using transfer learning"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes applying deep learning to decode skin niches and generating high-resolution representations via transfer learning, but does not specify a generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion. No clear foundation-model pretraining evidence is provided.""}",primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,none,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,generate high-resolution representations using transfer learning,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract describes applying deep learning to decode skin niches and generating high-resolution representations via transfer learning, but does not specify a generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion. No clear foundation-model pretraining evidence is provided.",EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""mixed_signals"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract reports a spatial multi-modal atlas and deep learning niche discovery without any natural-language text, text-bio bridge, or explicit generative mechanism; thus text component and bridge are missing.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,unclear,unclear,EC2_no_text_component,mixed_signals,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract reports a spatial multi-modal atlas and deep learning niche discovery without any natural-language text, text-bio bridge, or explicit generative mechanism; thus text component and bridge are missing.",EXCLUDE|UNCERTAIN,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,unclear,unclear,EC2_no_text_component,mixed_signals,none,none,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract reports a spatial multi-modal atlas and deep learning niche discovery without any natural-language text, text-bio bridge, or explicit generative mechanism; thus text component and bridge are missing.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,mixed_signals,False
