./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:241:3562,A Bilayer Feature Fusion Framework for Pan-Cancer Survival Prediction Based on Multihead Attention and Adaptive Differential Privacy: Model Development and Validation Study.,a bilayer feature fusion framework for pancancer survival prediction based on multihead attention and adaptive differential privacy model development and validation study,10.2196/83743,,41911549.0,,"In the field of precision medicine, pan-cancer survival prediction is crucial for individualized oncology diagnosis and treatment. Although multimodal data fusion techniques have significantly improved prediction accuracy, existing studies generally overlook the sensitivity of medical data and the need for privacy protection. To address the aforementioned problem, this study aims to propose a bilayer feature fusion framework based on the multihead attention mechanism and adaptive differential privacy, which balances precise feature extraction and sensitive data protection. Specifically, the multihead attention mechanism was integrated into bilayer feature extraction and fusion. Layer-wise relevance analysis was used to calculate the correlation between features and outcomes, and Laplacian noise was adaptively added based on the calculation results to achieve collaborative optimization of precise feature extraction and sensitive data protection. Additionally, the concordance index (C-index) and 5-fold cross-validation were used to compare the proposed method with state-of-the-art approaches. The proposed model achieved superior performance in both pan-cancer and single-cancer survival prediction, validated via the C-index and 5-fold cross-validation. In pan-cancer scenarios, the trimodal combination of clinical data, messenger RNA data, and microRNA expression data achieved the highest C-index of 0.799, outperforming multiple existing multimodal survival prediction approaches. After adaptive Laplacian noise injection for privacy protection, the model's accuracy decreased by only 0.01-0.03 while satisfying ϵ-differential privacy. For single-cancer prediction, the proposed method achieved higher C-index values in 18 out of 20 cancer types compared with representative deep learning-based survival models, with 7 types showing significant improvements (C-index difference >0.1) and more stable performance distributions. Furthermore, pan-cancer-trained models generally outperformed single-cancer-trained counterparts in most cancer types, highlighting the value of shared predictive features across cancers. This study provides a solution that balances prediction accuracy and privacy security for pan-cancer survival prediction, laying the foundation for the efficient use of medical data under privacy protection. Future work may further integrate pathological images and proteomics data to expand the model's applications in cancer subtype classification and biomarker discovery.",Chen Yun; Deng Zhifang; Wang Lili; Wang Huanhuan; Wu Xiang,2026.0,JMIR medical informatics,,,['pubmed'],1,['10.2196/83743'],1,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,107,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_include"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a bilayer feature fusion framework for survival prediction using clinical, mRNA, and microRNA data with multihead attention and differential privacy, without any natural‑language text component or text‑bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_include,"The abstract describes a bilayer feature fusion framework for survival prediction using clinical, mRNA, and microRNA data with multihead attention and differential privacy, without any natural‑language text component or text‑bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract proposes a multilayer attention-based framework for pan-cancer survival prediction, a predictive task, and contains no mention of decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, VAE/GAN, or any generative mechanism.""}",primary_model_paper,no,no,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract proposes a multilayer attention-based framework for pan-cancer survival prediction, a predictive task, and contains no mention of decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, VAE/GAN, or any generative mechanism.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,no,,,,,,,"The abstract describes a bilayer feature fusion framework for survival prediction using clinical, mRNA, and microRNA data with multihead attention and differential privacy, without any natural‑language text component or text‑bio bridge. | The abstract proposes a multilayer attention-based framework for pan-cancer survival prediction, a predictive task, and contains no mention of decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, VAE/GAN, or any generative mechanism.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:242:3563,Toward next-generation machine learning and deep learning for spatial omics.,toward nextgeneration machine learning and deep learning for spatial omics,10.1093/bib/bbag131,,41902503.0,,"Abstract Spatial omics technologies generate high-dimensional, spatially resolved molecular data across transcripts, proteins, metabolites and lipids, requiring computational models that account for tissue topology, multi-scale organization, and experimental noise. Although machine-learning (ML) and deep-learning (DL) methods have rapidly proliferated to meet these demands, the field still lacks clear methodological guidance for selecting models adapted to specific spatial constraints and biological questions. Here, we provide a critical and comparative synthesis of ML/DL approaches across core spatial omics tasks, including batch-effect correction, resolution enhancement, tissue and cell segmentation, spatial domain discovery, cell-type deconvolution, and model interpretability. Classical ML methods such as clustering, random forests, and other ensemble classifiers, offer interpretable baselines but are limited in their capacity to model non-linear spatial dependencies. Modern DL architectures, including convolutional and graph neural networks, transformers and generative models, capture complex spatial patterns and support multi-omics integration, yet face persistent challenges related to data scarcity, annotation burden, computational cost, and uncertainty estimation. Emerging strategies such as optimal transport, cross-modal attention, graph-linked embeddings, and foundation models enhance cross-modality alignment but require rigorous evaluation of their assumptions and operational constraints. We further discuss practical solutions, including self-supervised pretraining, federated learning and the adoption of standardized spatial data formats, to enhance scalability, reproducibility, and clinical readiness. Finally, we propose a decision framework that highlights when specific ML/DL families are most suitable according to data modality, spatial resolution, tissue architecture, and intended clinical application. By integrating methodological critique with actionable recommendations, this review offers a roadmap for the reproducible, interpretable, and clinically translatable deployment of ML and DL models in spatial omics.",Zirem Yanis; Fournier Isabelle; Salzet Michel,2026.0,Briefings in bioinformatics,,,"['google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.1093/bib/bbag131'],3,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,108,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""mixed_signals"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a review and comparative synthesis of ML/DL approaches for spatial omics, not a primary generative text-bio model.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,mixed_signals,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a review and comparative synthesis of ML/DL approaches for spatial omics, not a primary generative text-bio model.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a review that mentions generative models only as part of a survey and does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation‑model evidence for a candidate model.""}",review_editorial,no,unclear,review_editorial,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,The abstract is a review that mentions generative models only as part of a survey and does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation‑model evidence for a candidate model.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,no,unclear,review_editorial,mixed_signals,,,,,"The abstract describes a review and comparative synthesis of ML/DL approaches for spatial omics, not a primary generative text-bio model. | The abstract is a review that mentions generative models only as part of a survey and does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation‑model evidence for a candidate model.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,mixed_signals,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:243:3564,AI-Driven Drug Discovery: Focus on Targets for Solid Tumors.,aidriven drug discovery focus on targets for solid tumors,10.3390/pharmaceutics18030329,,41900815.0,,"In the field of anti-tumor drug development, target identification remains a key component of innovative therapeutic strategies. Solid malignancies have posed significant challenges to conventional target discovery approaches due to their distinct genetic heterogeneity, complex tumor microenvironment, and highly individualized evolutionary trajectories. In recent years, artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a revolutionary force in drug discovery. The technological advances from machine learning and deep learning to large language models (LLMs) has enabled the comprehensive integration and analysis of multi-omics biological data and real-world evidence, thereby promoting every stage of the drug discovery process. Thus, this article begins with an overview of the biological characteristics of tumors and the limitations of traditional strategies. It then delves into recent advances particularly in the past three years in the application of AI to drug discovery, especially LLMs. The main focus is on the current landscape of AI-assisted target identification. Furthermore, the article examines key challenges such as multimodal data integration and the interpretability of AI models, and envisions the future path towards integrated AI systems in precision oncology.",Wu Jialong; He Jide; Ni Qianyang; Li Zi'ang; Lin Xiushi; Zhao Zhenkun; Qiu Lei; Wang Hongyin; Li Sijie; Shi Chengdong; Zhang Yunyi; Gao Huile; Lu Jian,2026.0,Pharmaceutics,,,"['google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'scopus', 'semantic_scholar']",4,['10.3390/pharmaceutics18030329'],4,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,109,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a review of AI/LLM applications in drug discovery, not a primary generative text-bio model.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents a review of AI/LLM applications in drug discovery, not a primary generative text-bio model.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a review discussing LLMs in drug discovery without describing a new generative model or its architecture.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,The abstract is a review discussing LLMs in drug discovery without describing a new generative model or its architecture.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,,,,,,"The abstract presents a review of AI/LLM applications in drug discovery, not a primary generative text-bio model. | The abstract is a review discussing LLMs in drug discovery without describing a new generative model or its architecture.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:244:3565,"Molecular Pathology, Artificial Intelligence, and New Technologies in Hematologic Diagnostics: Translational Opportunities and Practical Considerations.",molecular pathology artificial intelligence and new technologies in hematologic diagnostics translational opportunities and practical considerations,10.3390/diagnostics16060913,,41897649.0,,"Background and Objectives: Diagnostics for hematologic diseases rely on integrated assessment of clinical manifestation, morphology, flow cytometry, and molecular testing. Current classification systems, including the WHO HAEM5 and the International Consensus Classification, highlight the central role of genomics in defining disease entities and risk. Simultaneously, laboratories face growing case complexity and staffing challenges. Automation, collaborative robots (cobots), digital morphology platforms, and artificial intelligence (AI) have begun to address these issues. Here we examine the application of these technologies in hematopathology and molecular diagnostics and consider their translational potential to improve diagnostic accuracy and, ultimately, patient care. Methods: A review of peer-reviewed literature and technical reports published through December 2025 was performed, focusing on digital morphology platforms, AI for peripheral blood and marrow interpretation, AI-enabled flow cytometry, automated and robotic deployments in clinical laboratories, and machine learning applications in molecular hematopathology. Results: Digital morphology analyzers show strong concordance with manual microscopy and now serve as efficient platforms for AI-assisted differentials, cell classification, and fibrosis quantification. Deep learning applied to multiparameter flow cytometry achieves performance comparable to expert review in distinguishing mature B-cell neoplasms and acute leukemias. Automated solutions, cobot systems and robotic-arm-based slide-scanning clusters have demonstrated substantial gains in throughput and pre-analytic consistency. AI models in molecular hematopathology increasingly assist with variant interpretation, genetic risk stratification, and linking morphologic and genomic findings. Conclusions: AI is beginning to change how hematopathology and molecular diagnostics are practiced. Successful translation will depend on disease-specific validation, the development of multi-modal models aligned with ICC and WHO frameworks, and laboratory governance that maintains expert oversight.",Alnoor Fnu; Mukherjee Shuvam; Menon Madhu P; Ng David; Li Peng; Ohgami Robert S,2026.0,"Diagnostics (Basel, Switzerland)",,,"['pubmed', 'scopus', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.3390/diagnostics16060913'],3,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,110,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a review of literature on AI in hematologic diagnostics, not a primary model.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a review of literature on AI in hematologic diagnostics, not a primary model.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a review of AI applications in hematopathology and does not describe any generative architecture or foundation-model evidence.""}",review_editorial,no,no,review_editorial,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,The abstract is a review of AI applications in hematopathology and does not describe any generative architecture or foundation-model evidence.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,unclear,unclear,unclear,no,no,review_editorial,,,,,,"The abstract describes a review of literature on AI in hematologic diagnostics, not a primary model. | The abstract is a review of AI applications in hematopathology and does not describe any generative architecture or foundation-model evidence.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:245:3566,Current Applications and Future Directions of Artificial Intelligence in Prostate Cancer Diagnosis: A Narrative Review.,current applications and future directions of artificial intelligence in prostate cancer diagnosis a narrative review,10.3390/curroncol33030166,,41892194.0,,"Prostate cancer (PCa) remains a major global health challenge, yet conventional diagnostic methods are often limited by suboptimal accuracy and efficiency. Artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a rapidly developing technology capable of integrating multi-source data to enhance clinical decision-making. This narrative review synthesizes current evidence regarding AI applications across key diagnostic domains, including medical imaging, digital pathology, liquid biopsy, and multi-omics integration. Findings indicate that AI models for magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can improve risk stratification and may reduce unnecessary biopsies in some cohorts, particularly when evaluated alongside structured radiology assessment and clinical variables. In digital pathology, deep learning algorithms have shown high agreement with expert genitourinary pathologists for automated Gleason grading in controlled and externally validated settings, with potential to reduce reporting time for high-volume workflows. Additionally, AI-powered liquid biopsy models may support non-invasive risk stratification, particularly for patients with prostate-specific antigen (PSA) levels in the diagnostic gray zone, while multi-omics integration is being investigated to enhance personalized assessment. Despite advances, challenges regarding data heterogeneity, algorithm interpretability, and workflow integration persist. Future research should prioritize multimodal data fusion, explainable AI development, robust calibration and decision-analytic evaluation, and large-scale prospective validation to standardize protocols and fully realize the potential of AI in precision prostate cancer care.",Zhu Cong-Yi; Qu Rui; Dai Yi; Yang Luo,2026.0,"Current oncology (Toronto, Ont.)",,,"['pubmed', 'scopus']",2,['10.3390/curroncol33030166'],2,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,111,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a narrative review of AI applications in prostate cancer diagnosis, not a primary model paper.""}",review_editorial,yes,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes a narrative review of AI applications in prostate cancer diagnosis, not a primary model paper.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a narrative review of AI applications in prostate cancer diagnosis and does not describe any generative model architecture or foundation model pretraining.""}",review_editorial,no,no,review_editorial,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,The abstract is a narrative review of AI applications in prostate cancer diagnosis and does not describe any generative model architecture or foundation model pretraining.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,yes,unclear,unclear,no,no,review_editorial,,,,,,"The abstract describes a narrative review of AI applications in prostate cancer diagnosis, not a primary model paper. | The abstract is a narrative review of AI applications in prostate cancer diagnosis and does not describe any generative model architecture or foundation model pretraining.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:246:3567,SpatialFusion: A lightweight multimodal foundation model for pathway-informed spatial niche mapping.,spatialfusion a lightweight multimodal foundation model for pathwayinformed spatial niche mapping,10.64898/2026.03.16.712056,,41890030.0,,"Foundation models enable knowledge transfer across data modalities and tasks, yet foundation models for spatial biology remain in their early stages, largely centered on encoding single-cell representations in spatial context without fully integrating transcriptomic and morphological information to delineate functional niches. Here we introduce SpatialFusion, a lightweight multimodal foundation model that identifies biologically coherent microenvironments defined by distinct pathway activation patterns rather than spatial proximity alone. SpatialFusion integrates paired histopathology, gene expression, and inferred pathway activity into a unified representation. Compared with two specialist niche-detection methods and four spatial foundation models, SpatialFusion performs competitively and consistently resolves fine-grained spatial niches with unique pathway-level signatures. Applying the model to two Visium HD cohorts uncovered a pre-malignant niche in morphologically normal mucosa adjacent to colorectal tumors and revealed distinct malignant microenvironments in non-small cell lung cancer that were predictive of tumor stage. Overall, SpatialFusion offers a versatile framework for multimodal spatial analysis, enabling the discovery of new morpho-molecular niches with significant biological and clinical relevance.",Yates Josephine; Shavakhi Mitra; Choueiri Toni K; Van Allen Eliezer M; Uhler Caroline,2026.0,bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology,,,"['google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",3,['10.64898/2026.03.16.712056'],3,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,112,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents SpatialFusion as a multimodal foundation model integrating histopathology, gene expression, and inferred pathway activity, with no mention of natural language text or text-bio alignment.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,none,none,none,none,not_assessed,none,"The abstract presents SpatialFusion as a multimodal foundation model integrating histopathology, gene expression, and inferred pathway activity, with no mention of natural language text or text-bio alignment.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes SpatialFusion as a lightweight multimodal foundation model that integrates histopathology, gene expression, and pathway activity into a unified representation for niche identification, without mentioning any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes SpatialFusion as a lightweight multimodal foundation model that integrates histopathology, gene expression, and pathway activity into a unified representation for niche identification, without mentioning any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,,,,,,,"The abstract presents SpatialFusion as a multimodal foundation model integrating histopathology, gene expression, and inferred pathway activity, with no mention of natural language text or text-bio alignment. | The abstract describes SpatialFusion as a lightweight multimodal foundation model that integrates histopathology, gene expression, and pathway activity into a unified representation for niche identification, without mentioning any generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:247:3568,"HViLM: A Foundation Model for Viral Genomics Enables Multi-Task Prediction of Pathogenicity, Transmissibility, and Host Tropism.",hvilm a foundation model for viral genomics enables multitask prediction of pathogenicity transmissibility and host tropism,10.64898/2026.03.18.712700,,41890006.0,,"The emergence of novel viral pathogens poses critical threats to global health, yet current computational approaches for viral risk assessment are predominantly virus-specific and require extensive retraining for each new threat. Computational methods for rapid characterization of emerging viruses across multiple epidemiologically relevant dimensions-pathogenicity, host tropism, and transmissibility-are urgently needed to inform public health responses and guide experimental prioritization. We present  The HVUE benchmark datasets, training scripts, and complete implementation are publicly available at https://github.com/duttaprat/HViLM . Pre-trained HViLM-base model weights and fine-tuned task-specific variants are available on Hugging Face at https://huggingface.co/duttaprat/HViLM-base . Supplementary data are available online.",Dutta Pratik; Vaska Jack; Surana Pallavi; Sathian Rekha; Chao Max; Zhou Zhihan; Liu Han; Davuluri Ramana V,2026.0,bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology,,,"['pubmed', 'semantic_scholar']",2,['10.64898/2026.03.18.712700'],2,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,113,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""unclear"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""text_component_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a viral genomics foundation model for predicting pathogenicity, transmissibility, and host tropism but contains no mention of natural language text or any text-bio bridging mechanism.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,unclear,unclear,none,text_component_unclear,none,none,not_assessed,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract presents a viral genomics foundation model for predicting pathogenicity, transmissibility, and host tropism but contains no mention of natural language text or any text-bio bridging mechanism.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents HViLM as a foundation model for viral genomics that learns from sequences and is fine-tuned for prediction tasks, but it does not mention any decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, or other generative mechanism.""}",primary_model_paper,no,yes,none,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents HViLM as a foundation model for viral genomics that learns from sequences and is fine-tuned for prediction tasks, but it does not mention any decoder, autoregressive, sequence-to-sequence, diffusion, or other generative mechanism.",UNCERTAIN,EXCLUDE,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""text_component_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""Title asserts a foundation model for viral genomics, but the abstract lacks any mention of natural language text, text-bio alignment, or generative architecture, so the paper fails the text component criterion.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,none,text_component_boundary,"Title asserts a foundation model for viral genomics, but the abstract lacks any mention of natural language text, text-bio alignment, or generative architecture, so the paper fails the text component criterion.",UNCERTAIN|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,yes,EC2_no_text_component,,none,none,none,text_component_boundary,"Title asserts a foundation model for viral genomics, but the abstract lacks any mention of natural language text, text-bio alignment, or generative architecture, so the paper fails the text component criterion.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:249:                Cancer heterogeneity poses a major challenge for accurate molecular subtype classification. Conventional methods often fail to exploit complementary information across multiple omics modalities, leading to overfitting on high-dimensional data and limited representation of subtype heterogeneity. To address this, we propose Triad-LMF, a multiomics integration framework based on low-rank multimodal fusion to improve classification accuracy. Triad-LMF harmonizes heterogeneous omics data and integrates information through a two-stage hierarchical fusion strategy. Local Pairwise Fusion and Global Triadic Fusion are combined via the Two-Feature and Three-Way LMF modules, enabling a gradual transition from local modality interactions to global feature integration. Experimental results show that Triad-LMF consistently outperforms existing methods. UMAP visualization confirms enhanced subtype separability, and SHAP-based analysis highlights biologically meaningful features. Across independent datasets, Triad-LMF demonstrates strong generalization, offering a robust and interpretable framework for multiomics-driven cancer subtype classification.",Tan Xingyue; Chen Xiran; Tian Renjie; Cai Qinyu; Jiang Miaoyuan; Yang Dongqiu; Zhang Lei,2026.0,BMC bioinformatics,,,['pubmed'],1,['10.1186/s12859-026-06378-3'],1,pubmed,crossref_title,85.0,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,114,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents a multi-omics classification framework without any text or language component, so it lacks a substantive text component and text-bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents a multi-omics classification framework without any text or language component, so it lacks a substantive text component and text-bio bridge.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""no"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC3_not_generative"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents Triad-LMF as a low-rank multimodal fusion framework for cancer subtype classification, focusing on classification accuracy and representation learning, with no description of generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or sequence-to-sequence generation.""}",primary_model_paper,no,no,EC3_not_generative,none,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents Triad-LMF as a low-rank multimodal fusion framework for cancer subtype classification, focusing on classification accuracy and representation learning, with no description of generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or sequence-to-sequence generation.",EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,,,,,,"The abstract presents a multi-omics classification framework without any text or language component, so it lacks a substantive text component and text-bio bridge. | The abstract presents Triad-LMF as a low-rank multimodal fusion framework for cancer subtype classification, focusing on classification accuracy and representation learning, with no description of generative mechanisms such as decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or sequence-to-sequence generation.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:250:3570,High-fidelity bidirectional translation between single-cell transcriptomes and DNA methylomes with scBOND.,highfidelity bidirectional translation between singlecell transcriptomes and dna methylomes with scbond,10.1101/gr.281350.125,,41887797.0,,"Single-cell multiomic sequencing technologies have offered unprecedented insights into cellular heterogeneity by jointly profiling gene expression and epigenetic landscapes at single-cell resolution. However, the application of these technologies remains limited owing to technical challenges and high costs. Computational approaches for cross-modality translation provide a promising solution to these limitations by enabling the inference of one modality from another. However, existing methods for cross-modality translation between single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) and single-cell DNA methylation (scDNAm) data face limitations, including unidirectionality, inadequate modeling of context-specific DNA methylation-expression associations, neglect of biological relevance in evaluation, and poor performance in limited paired training data. To fill these gaps, we introduce scBOND, a bidirectional cross-modal translation framework tailored for scRNA-seq and scDNAm profiles. scBOND leverages a mixture-of-experts block to capture context-dependent regulatory patterns, while implementing self-attention mechanism and a feature recalibration module to enhance biological signal fidelity. Extensive experiments demonstrate scBOND consistently outperforms baseline methods in both translation directions, yielding high-accuracy translation while preserving cellular structure. In mouse embryonic data, scBOND preserves subtle, functionally significant differences between closely related cell types, which are undetected in the original data. Downstream analyses confirm that scBOND effectively recovers tissue-specific signals in human brain neurons. Moreover, using RNA-only data, we reconstruct scDNAm profiles and identify cell-type- and stage-specific regulatory mechanisms in oligodendrocyte lineage. To further improve model generalization in paired data-scarce scenarios, we propose scBOND-Aug, a variant of scBOND equipped with a biologically informed data augmentation strategy, which demonstrates superior results with limited paired data.",Lang Kehan; Jia Chenyang; Li Siyu; Guo Yi; Hu Dingjun; Chen Shengquan,2026.0,Genome research,,,['pubmed'],1,['10.1101/gr.281350.125'],1,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,115,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents scBOND as a bidirectional cross-modal translation framework for single‑cell RNA‑seq and DNA methylation data, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, captions, or language‑model components.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract presents scBOND as a bidirectional cross-modal translation framework for single‑cell RNA‑seq and DNA methylation data, with no mention of natural‑language text, prompts, captions, or language‑model components.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a bidirectional translation framework using self-attention and mixture-of-experts but does not explicitly mention a generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion; thus generative status is unclear and no foundation-model evidence is provided.""}",primary_model_paper,unclear,no,none,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract describes a bidirectional translation framework using self-attention and mixture-of-experts but does not explicitly mention a generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, or diffusion; thus generative status is unclear and no foundation-model evidence is provided.",EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""single-cell transcriptomes and DNA methylomes"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""bidirectional cross-modal translation framework tailored for scRNA-seq and scDNAm profiles"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes a model for translating between single-cell transcriptomes and DNA methylomes without any natural-language text, so text component and text-bio bridge are absent. It does not explicitly state a generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion), leaving generative status uncertain.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,unclear,no,EC2_no_text_component,generative_status_unclear,single-cell transcriptomes and DNA methylomes,bidirectional cross-modal translation framework tailored for scRNA-seq and scDNAm profiles,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract describes a model for translating between single-cell transcriptomes and DNA methylomes without any natural-language text, so text component and text-bio bridge are absent. It does not explicitly state a generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion), leaving generative status uncertain.",EXCLUDE|UNCERTAIN,primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,unclear,no,EC2_no_text_component,generative_status_unclear,single-cell transcriptomes and DNA methylomes,bidirectional cross-modal translation framework tailored for scRNA-seq and scDNAm profiles,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract describes a model for translating between single-cell transcriptomes and DNA methylomes without any natural-language text, so text component and text-bio bridge are absent. It does not explicitly state a generative mechanism (e.g., decoder, autoregressive, diffusion), leaving generative status uncertain.",EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,generative_status_unclear,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:251:3571,"Large language model agents for biological intelligence across genomics, proteomics, spatial biology, and biomedicine.",large language model agents for biological intelligence across genomics proteomics spatial biology and biomedicine,10.1093/bib/bbag110,,41883029.0,,"Large language models (LLMs) are evolving from passive predictors into agentic systems capable of planning, tool-use, and multimodal reasoning. This shift is especially consequential for biology, where complex, noisy, and multi-scale data require adaptive and integrative computational strategies. In this review, we provide the first systematic synthesis of LLM-based agents across genomics, molecular biology, imaging, biomedical analysis, and automated bioinformatics workflows. We analyze >60 emerging systems and organize them within a unifying framework that characterizes agentic traits, such as autonomous decision-making, external tool invocation, memory, and self-correction. Across domains, agentic LLMs show early promise in enabling multi-step analysis, linking heterogeneous evidence, and supporting exploratory scientific tasks. At the same time, our comparative assessment highlights consistent challenges, including unstable reasoning, limited biological grounding, retrieval misalignment, and barriers to reproducibility and biosafety. We conclude by outlining opportunities for trustworthy and collaborative biological agents, including multimodal integration, closed-loop experimental design, and robust evaluation practices. This survey aims to clarify the emerging landscape and chart a path toward reliable agentic systems for biological discovery.",Dip Sajib Acharjee; Mallick Dipanwita; Acharjee Shuvo Uddip; Barua Soumma Shovito; Rafsani Fazle; Kumar Paul Bikash; Ahmed Moumi Nazifa; Ahmed Shafayat; Zhang Liqing,2026.0,Briefings in bioinformatics,,,"['google_scholar', 'pubmed', 'scopus']",3,['10.1093/bib/bbag110'],3,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,116,"{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""yes"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""yes"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""Large language model agents"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""LLM-based agents across genomics, molecular biology, imaging, biomedical analysis, and automated bioinformatics workflows"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""none"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The paper reviews LLM-based agents applied to biological domains, showing LLMs as a text component integrated with genomics, proteomics, spatial biology, and biomedicine.""}",review_editorial,yes,yes,yes,review_editorial,none,Large language model agents,"LLM-based agents across genomics, molecular biology, imaging, biomedical analysis, and automated bioinformatics workflows",not_assessed,none,"The paper reviews LLM-based agents applied to biological domains, showing LLMs as a text component integrated with genomics, proteomics, spatial biology, and biomedicine.","{""paper_type"": ""review_editorial"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""review_editorial"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract is a review/survey of LLM-based agents and does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation-model evidence for a candidate model.""}",review_editorial,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,clear_exclude,The abstract is a review/survey of LLM-based agents and does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation-model evidence for a candidate model.,EXCLUDE,EXCLUDE,False,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,EXCLUDE|EXCLUDE,review_editorial,yes,yes,yes,unclear,unclear,review_editorial,,,,,,"The paper reviews LLM-based agents applied to biological domains, showing LLMs as a text component integrated with genomics, proteomics, spatial biology, and biomedicine. | The abstract is a review/survey of LLM-based agents and does not describe a new generative architecture or provide foundation-model evidence for a candidate model.",EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,EXCLUDE,review_editorial,,False
./analysis/screening_run_artifacts_after_prompt_update_20260511/full_runs/nemotron3_super_120b_fp8_review_pipeline_all4027_current_watchdog_20260511T1116Z/job_11581/guideline_pilot_results_partial.csv:252:3572,Toward Computationally Complete Spatial Omics.,toward computationally complete spatial omics,10.64898/2026.03.03.709303,,41867863.0,,"Multimodal spatial omics has transformed biology by mapping molecular complexity within intact tissues, yet current technologies remain limited in the number of modalities measured simultaneously and often produce lower-quality data than single-modality assays. We present COSIE, a computational framework that generates high-resolution, multilayered molecular landscapes across tissue sections, individuals, and platforms. COSIE integrates histology, epigenome, transcriptome, proteome, and metabolome into a unified representation. Applied to 12 datasets spanning 10 spatial technologies, eight modalities, and nine tissue types, ranging from thousands of spots to millions of cells, COSIE outperforms existing methods. It resolves tissue structures, enhances noisy measurements, predicts unmeasured modalities, and captures dynamic processes. In human tumors, COSIE identifies invasive subregions linked to clinical outcomes and predicts spatial gene expression in TCGA samples using only histology images. By transforming fragmented data into comprehensive spatial maps, COSIE advances computationally complete spatial omics and the creation of digital tissue twins for biomedicine.",Li Wei; Mao Liran; Liu Yunhe; Peng Fuduan; Sachs Nadja; Wu Wenrui; Yiu Stephanie Pei Tung; Yan Hanying; Schroeder Amelia; Yu Xiaokang; Jin Kaitian; Jiang Shunzhou; Chen Zihao; Loth Melanie L; Gomez Lorena; Lubo Idania; Blank Niklas; Samarah Laith Z; Basak Ankit; Cho Ye Won; Chen Chia-Yu; Kim David M; Shalek Alex K; Soto Luisa Maren Solis; Rabinowitz Joshua D; Reilly Muredach P; Qian Xuyu; Thaiss Christoph A; Maegdefessel Lars; Wang Linghua; Kadara Humam; Jiang Sizun; Deng Yanxiang; Li Mingyao,2026.0,bioRxiv : the preprint server for biology,,,['pubmed'],1,['10.64898/2026.03.03.709303'],1,,,,2026-03-01_to_2026-04-14,117,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""not_assessed"", ""boundary_case"": ""clear_exclude"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes COSIE as a computational framework integrating histology, epigenome, transcriptome, proteome, and metabolome but does not mention any text or language component or text-bio bridging.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,EC2_no_text_component,none,none,none,not_assessed,clear_exclude,"The abstract describes COSIE as a computational framework integrating histology, epigenome, transcriptome, proteome, and metabolome but does not mention any text or language component or text-bio bridging.","{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""none"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""not_assessed"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract presents COSIE as a framework that generates molecular landscapes and predicts modalities, but does not specify a generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or VAE/GAN, leaving its generative status unclear.""}",primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,none,generative_status_unclear,not_assessed,not_assessed,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract presents COSIE as a framework that generates molecular landscapes and predicts modalities, but does not specify a generative mechanism such as decoder, autoregressive, diffusion, or VAE/GAN, leaving its generative status unclear.",EXCLUDE,UNCERTAIN,True,"{""paper_type"": ""primary_model_paper"", ""bio_modality_present"": ""yes"", ""text_component_present"": ""no"", ""text_bio_bridge_present"": ""no"", ""generative_model_present"": ""unclear"", ""foundation_model_evidence"": ""unclear"", ""primary_exclusion_code"": ""EC2_no_text_component"", ""uncertainty_reason"": ""generative_status_unclear"", ""evidence_for_text_component"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_text_bio_bridge"": ""none"", ""evidence_for_generative_model"": ""none"", ""boundary_case"": ""thin_abstract_boundary"", ""decision_rationale"": ""The abstract describes COSIE generating spatial omics landscapes but does not specify a generative mechanism, leaving generative status unclear; it contains no natural language text or text-bio bridge.""}",primary_model_paper,yes,no,no,unclear,unclear,EC2_no_text_component,generative_status_unclear,none,none,none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract describes COSIE generating spatial omics landscapes but does not specify a generative mechanism, leaving generative status unclear; _bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"EC2_no_text_component","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The study uses genomic data and imaging for lung cancer prediction, but the abstract does not describe any language or text component. It is a multimodal bio-imaging model, not a text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"integrates genomic data and imaging\" and \"hybrid deep learning model\""},{"record_id":"rec_001189","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a minireview on AI in gastric diseases rather than a primary model paper. It discusses multimodal clinical AI and large language models in general.","evidence_snippet":"\"This minireview addresses\" and \"transition from domain-specific deep learning to general-purpose large language models\""},{"record_id":"rec_001190","paper_type":"application_wrapper","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"yes","primary_exclusion_code":"application_wrapper","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The abstract applies an existing reasoning LLM to scRNAseq cell type annotation using prompts over marker genes and expressed genes. That is an application of a pre-existing model rather than a new candidate model.","evidence_snippet":"\"employing DeepSeek-R1... for zero-shot scRNAseq cell type annotation\" and \"prompted with a ranked list of... marker genes\""},{"record_id":"rec_001191","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"The paper presents a multimodal atlas/resource for enhancer and disease interpretation, not a generative text-bio foundation model. No substantive text or language component is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"cell type–resolved multimodal atlas\" and \"systematically interpret genetic variants\""},{"record_id":"rec_001192","paper_type":"benchmark_resource","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"no","text_bio_bridge_present":"no","primary_exclusion_code":"benchmark_resource","uncertainty_reason":"none","decision_rationale":"This is a benchmarking paper for self-supervised learning on single-cell data, not a primary model paper. The abstract focuses on evaluating methods and downstream tasks.","evidence_snippet":"\"we present scSSL-Bench, a comprehensive benchmark that evaluates nineteen SSL methods\""}]}
