./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0136.stderr.log:2815:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10_pilot2/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0001.stderr.log:37:- text_bio_bridge_present: yes | no | unclear
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0136.stderr.log:2818:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10_pilot2/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0001.stderr.log:51:- If the abstract only supports wrapper-style use of an existing LLM or language model, set `paper_type=application_wrapper` and `text_bio_bridge_present=no`.
./data/screening_codex_full_2026-07-06/role_logs/scope_reviewer/batch_0136.stderr.log:2819:./data/screening_codex_2026-06-10_pilot2/role_logs/adjudicator/batch_0001.stderr.log:66:[{"record_id":"rec_000001","title":"The translational paradox of AI in hepatocellular carcinoma: from algorithmic over-engineering to real-world clinical utility.","abstract":"Synthesizing recent literature from 2025 to 2026, this mini-review critically evaluates the methodological evolution of artificial intelligence (AI) in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) from static pattern recognition to mechanism-driven spatial and imaging diagnostics. We expose a critical translational paradox: although self-supervised Vision Foundation Models (VFMs), unsupervised domain adaptation for H&E-based virtual molecular profiling, and AI-synergized spatial transcriptomics theoretically decode intratumoral heterogeneity and mitigate cross-etiology domain shifts, these complex architectures remain unvalidated in real-world multi-scanner cohorts. Conversely, robust comparative analyses challenge the prevailing multi-modal narrative by demonstrating that algorithmic utility is highly data-dependent: although complex AI is crucial for high-dimensional settings, traditional Cox models remain highly robust and competitive compared to highly engineered large language models (LLMs) in low-dimensional survival prediction, indicating that algorithmic complexity does not inevitably equal clinical utility. To break this translational impasse, this mini-review argues that the field must urgently pivot away from fragile post-hoc explainability toward inherently interpretable architectures—such as concept bottleneck models (CBMs)—and calls for the rigorous integration of these transparent systems into Phase II “window-of-opportunity” randomized trials and dynamic regulatory evaluation frameworks within the next 24 months.","doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1848190","year":"2026","venue":"Frontiers in oncology","sources":["pubmed","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000001","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly says this is a \"mini-review\" synthesizing literature, not a primary model paper. It discusses VFMs, spatial transcriptomics, and LLMs as reviewed examples rather than presenting a candidate text-bio model.","evidence_snippet":"\"this mini-review critically evaluates\"; mentions \"Vision Foundation Models (VFMs)\" and \"large language models (LLMs)\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000001","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"This is a mini-review synthesizing prior literature, not a primary model paper. It mentions VFMs and LLMs as examples in the field, but no new generative architecture or foundation-model training is described.","evidence_snippet":"\"Synthesizing recent literature\"; mentions \"self-supervised Vision Foundation Models (VFMs)\" and \"highly engineered large language models (LLMs)\" in a review context."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}},{"record_id":"rec_000002","title":"Evaluating the Role of AI Assistants in Accelerating Neurodegenerative Disease Research: Opportunities and Translational Limitations.","abstract":"Abstract Neurodegenerative diseases including Alzheimer’s disease and Parkinson’s disease remain among the most challenging disorders to study, diagnose and treat. Despite rising prevalence with population aging, disease-modifying therapies remain scarce and research progress is hindered by biological complexity, patient heterogeneity, and incomplete experimental systems. Artificial intelligence (AI) has emerged as a transformative approach given the high-dimensional and multimodal data generated in this field. Traditional machine learning and deep learning have advanced imaging biomarker detection, disease trajectory prediction, drug target prioritization, and clinical data mining. More recently, foundation models and large language models (LLMs) have expanded AI from task-specific prediction tools to versatile assistants supporting literature retrieval, summarization, coding, data interpretation, and hypothesis generation. Although AI assistants promise to accelerate research workflows, their outputs are prone to dataset biases, poor interpretability, distribution shift, and hallucinations, which are particularly problematic in neurodegenerative research given subtle phenotypic variations, imperfect labeling, and protracted disease courses. This review evaluates the current applications of AI in neurodegenerative disease research, including drug discovery, biomarker identification, and multi-omics integration. We then discuss the transition from analytical AI models to general-purpose AI assistants and their potential to streamline scientific workflows. Critical limitations including bias, interpretability, reproducibility, and LLM hallucinations are highlighted, alongside ethical, regulatory and practical challenges. We argue that the most sustainable near-term model is human-AI collaboration rather than fully autonomous research, with its primary focus placed on research rather than clinical practice. Meaningful acceleration requires rigorous validation, transparent usage, and expert oversight to preserve scientific rigor and translational relevance.","doi":"10.2147/NDT.S614952","year":"2026","venue":"Neuropsychiatric disease and treatment","sources":["pubmed","semantic_scholar"],"first_pass_outputs":{"scope_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000002","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"text_bio_bridge_unclear","decision_rationale":"The abstract states that this paper \"evaluates the current applications of AI\" and is a review of AI in neurodegenerative disease research, so it is not a primary model paper. It mentions foundation models and LLMs as tools for research workflows rather than a substantive text-bio model bridge.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review evaluates the current applications of AI\"; \"foundation models and large language models (LLMs)\""},"architecture_reviewer":{"record_id":"rec_000002","paper_type":"review_editorial","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear","primary_exclusion_code":"review_editorial","uncertainty_reason":"mixed_signals","decision_rationale":"The abstract explicitly frames the article as a review of AI assistants in neurodegenerative disease research. Foundation models and LLMs are discussed as existing tools, but no candidate generative model is presented or characterized.","evidence_snippet":"\"This review evaluates\" and discusses \"foundation models and large language models (LLMs)\" as research tools."},"python_gate":{"provisional_decision":"ADJUDICATE","provisional_code":"review_editorial","adjudication_reason":"criterion_unclear_or_reviewer_conflict","paper_type":"review_editorial","bio_modality_present":"yes","text_component_present":"yes","text_bio_bridge_present":"unclear","generative_model_present":"unclear","foundation_model_evidence":"unclear"}}}]
